{"chunk_id": "7f7a5933900b", "text": "This passage tells a mythic explanation for why death exists. The maiden called Death is commanded by Brahman to destroy living creatures, but she recoils in horror, fearing the grief of families and the sin of killing. Refusing the task, she performs unimaginably long austerities across sacred places\u2014standing on one foot for billions of years, living on air, water, or silence\u2014in hopes of being released from the command. Brahman insists that destruction is necessary to the cosmic order and promises she will incur no sin. Her tears become diseases that eventually bring about death, while Desire and Wrath help end lives when their time comes. The story teaches that death is part of divine order and that grieving should be tempered by the knowledge of rebirth and the soul\u2019s continuing journey.\nThe goddess Death resists killing beings until Brahma assures her it is righteous; diseases arise from her tears and death becomes part of cosmic order.\nThemes: origin of death, cosmic duty, austerity and penance, disease as divine manifestation, cycle of rebirth", "vector": [0.005946319084614515, -0.010088328272104263, -0.012584070675075054, 0.0016849549720063806, -0.010292465798556805, 0.015435406006872654, -0.028895290568470955, -0.004691862966865301, 0.0008684062049724162, -0.008540837094187737, 0.017318736761808395, 0.015145663172006607, 0.012452368624508381, 0.0037666605785489082, -0.008178657852113247, 0.004244077950716019, 0.04983581602573395, 0.003486794885247946, 0.031344935297966, -0.008172072470188141, 0.004919047933071852, 0.017187034711241722, 0.004072866402566433, 0.029632817953824997, -0.012300912290811539, 0.018754281103610992, 0.013631096109747887, -0.034268707036972046, 0.01272894162684679, -0.003605326171964407, -0.006213014479726553, -0.013097705319523811, -0.020782483741641045, -0.016462676227092743, -0.03440041095018387, 7.104674295987934e-05, -0.007157972082495689, -0.017832372337579727, 0.006588363088